On Saturday, April 21, 2012 3:58:34 PM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:<br><blockquote class="gmail_quote" style="margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex">Here&#39;s the current dot_product in stan/math/matrix.hpp:
<br>
<br>     template&lt;int R1,int C1,int R2, int C2&gt;
<br>     inline double dot_product(const Eigen::Matrix&lt;double, R1, C1&gt;&amp; v1,
<br>                               const Eigen::Matrix&lt;double, R2, C2&gt;&amp; v2) {
<br>       if (v1.rows() != 1 &amp;&amp; v1.cols() != 1)
<br>         throw std::invalid_argument(&quot;v1 must be a vector&quot;);
<br>       if (v2.rows() != 1 &amp;&amp; v2.cols() != 1)
<br>         throw std::invalid_argument(&quot;v2 must be a vector&quot;);
<br>       if (v1.size() != v2.size())
<br>         throw std::invalid_argument(&quot;v1.size() must equal v2.size()&quot;);
<br>       return v1.dot(v2);
<br>     }
<br>
<br>Issue 1
<br>-------
<br>This only compiles if one of R1 and C1 is 1
<br>and one of R2 and C2 is 1, so the dynamic vector checks
<br>are redundant (they&#39;ll always pass if the code compiled).
<br></blockquote>
